"Explores directional influences among problem factors to identify promising drivers and outcomes for further investigation.\n\nUse when: Examine relationships across themes from an affinity exercise. Find influential factors in a complex improvement problem.\n\nAvoid when: The team needs formal feedback polarity, dynamic simulation or proven causal attribution.\n\nVisual grammar: Factor cards name distinct aspects of one scoped issue. Directed arrows represent a stated influence from one factor to another. Incoming and outgoing counts may guide discussion without constituting causal proof.\n\nBuild: State the issue and choose concise, nonoverlapping factors. Examine factor pairs for a defensible influence relationship. Review potential drivers and effects, using focused views or a matrix for dense links.\n\nCheck: Arrow direction follows the stated influence. Factors are not merely differently worded copies. High arrow counts are clues for inquiry rather than an automatic priority rule.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing two directions because the team has not resolved the relationship. Deleting relationships merely to achieve a neat layout.\n\nExample: A community service connects unclear ownership, slow decisions, rework and delayed responses to choose a focused investigation.\n\nAlternatives: Fishbone diagram, Causal-loop diagram."
